  "response": "**VERDICT: REJECT**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The `BuggyToast` component fails at a foundational level for assistive technology users. It relies entirely on visual presentation with zero programmatic state communication, no keyboard dismissal mechanism, and no focus management on mount. This is not a styling or enhancement gap; it is a complete access barrier for screen reader and keyboard-only users. The design must be revised before implementation can proceed.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**: \nBefore reading the code, I predicted: (1) missing `aria-live`/`role=\"alert\"` causing silent toast for AT users, (2) no keyboard dismiss handler despite auto-dismissal, (3) focus not moving to the toast on mount, (4) visual-only type indicators violating color-as-sole-indicator rules, and (5) 3-second auto-dismissal lacking pause/resume control. All predictions were confirmed by direct code inspection.\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n1. **Missing programmatic state communication for dynamic content**\n   - `BuggyToast.jsx:20` renders `{message}` inside a plain `<div className=\"toast-notification\">` with zero ARIA attributes or live region configuration.\n   - **User group impacted**: Screen reader users (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver) will never hear the toast unless they manually navigate to it while it's visible.\n   - **Expected behavior**: Per WAI-ARIA APG Alert pattern & WCAG 4.1.3 Status Messages, dynamic notifications must use `aria-live=\"assertive\"` (or `role=\"alert\"`) with `aria-atomic=\"true\"` to ensure immediate, complete announcement.\n   - **Fix**: Replace `<div>` with `<div aria-live=\"assertive\" aria-atomic=\"true\" role=\"status\">` or `<div role=\"alert\">`. Ensure the live region is mounted before React renders it (avoid conditional mounting that destroys the region).\n\n2. **No keyboard dismiss mechanism**\n   - `BuggyToast.jsx:7-13` implements a timer-based auto-dismissal but provides no interactive element or key handler for manual dismissal.\n   - **User group impacted**: Keyboard-only users, cognitive accessibility users who need more time to read, and users with motor impairments who cannot rely on automatic timeouts.\n   - **Expected behavior**: Per WCAG 2.2.2 Pause/Stop/Hide & WAI-ARIA APG Alert pattern, dynamic content that auto-dismisses must provide a keyboard-accessible dismiss control (e.g., `<button aria-label=\"Dismiss notification\">`). The timer should pause on hover/focus and resume on blur.\n   - **Fix**: Add a dismiss button inside the toast. Implement `onFocus`/`onMouseEnter` to clear the timer, and `onBlur`/`onMouseLeave` to restart it. Ensure the button has visible focus indicators meeting WCAG 2.4.7.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n1. **Focus management on mount is absent**\n   - `BuggyToast.jsx:19-22` mounts a new DOM node but does not move focus to it or announce its presence programmatically beyond live regions.\n   - **User group impacted**: Screen reader users relying on focus tracking, and keyboard users who expect dynamic content to become the active context.\n   - **Expected behavior**: Per WCAG 2.4.3 Focus Order & APG Alert pattern, when a toast appears, focus should either move to it (if it requires interaction) or remain on the trigger element while the live region announces. If focus moves, it must be managed via `useRef` and `focus()` with proper timing to avoid React unmount race conditions.\n   - **Fix**: If dismissal is required, move focus to the dismiss button on mount (`toastRef.current?.querySelector('button')?.focus()`). If non-interactive, keep focus on the trigger and rely solely on `aria-live`.\n\n2. **Toast type/state not communicated programmatically**\n   - Expected behavior states: \"Visual indicator shows toast type (success, error, etc.)\". The component accepts no `type` prop and has no programmatic mapping for success/warning/error states.\n   - **User group impacted**: Low vision & color-blind users; screen reader users who need context about the notification's nature.\n   - **Expected behavior**: Per WCAG 1.4.1 Use of Color, state must not rely on color alone. Programmatic state should be exposed via `aria-label` or `role=\"alert\"` with explicit type text (e.g., `\"Success: Your profile was updated.\"`).\n   - **Fix**: Add a `type` prop. Map it to accessible labels (`{type === 'success' && <span aria-hidden=\"true\">\u2713 </span> Success:`) and ensure the live region announces the full string including type context.\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n- **Auto-dismissal timing lacks adjustability**: `duration = 3000` is fixed. Per WCAG 2.2.1 Timing Adjustable, users should be able to extend or pause dynamic timeouts. Suggest exposing a `minDuration` prop or providing a \"Pause\" control.\n- **Missing `aria-describedby` for context**: If the toast relates to a specific action (e.g., form submission), it should reference the triggering element via `aria-describedby` so screen readers understand the relationship.\n\n**Enhancements** (best practice not met):\n- Consider using `react-hot-toast` or similar battle-tested libraries that handle live region mounting, focus management, and reduced-motion preferences out of the box.\n- Add `prefers-reduced-motion` handling to suppress entrance animations for vestibular users.\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n- **Live region lifecycle**: Conditional rendering (`if (!isVisible) return null`) destroys and recreates the live region on every dismiss/appear cycle. This can cause screen readers to lose track of announcements or fail to read them reliably. The live region should be mounted once in a parent container with `aria-live` applied, and content should be updated via state/props instead of unmounting.\n- **Keyboard trap risk**: If focus moves to the toast but no dismiss button exists, keyboard users are trapped until the timer fires.\n- **ARIA label for screen reader context**: No `aria-label` or visible heading distinguishes this from other page content. Screen readers won't know it's a notification without live region semantics.\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | HIGH | Zero `aria-live`/`role=\"alert\"`; conditional mounting breaks live region stability |\n| Keyboard-only | HIGH | No dismiss control; focus management undefined; auto-dismissal traps until timeout |\n| Low vision | MEDIUM | Visual type indicators likely rely on color alone (WCAG 1.4.1 risk) |\n| Cognitive | MEDIUM | Fixed 3s timeout without pause/resume; no undo or clear action confirmation |\n| Vestibular & motion | LOW | No animation code present, but should be explicitly suppressed if added later |\n| Auditory access | LOW | Not applicable (no media elements) |\n| Environmental contrast | MEDIUM | Color-dependent toast types will fail in forced-colors/high-contrast modes without programmatic fallback |\n\n**Verdict Justification**: The component fails at the core accessibility contract: dynamic content must be programmatically announced to assistive technology. Without `aria-live` or `role=\"alert\"`, screen reader users receive zero feedback. Without a keyboard dismiss control, keyboard users are forced to wait for an arbitrary timeout or navigate away. The design is fundamentally incomplete per WAI-ARIA APG Alert/Toast patterns and WCAG 2.2 criteria 4.1.3, 2.2.2, and 1.4.1. These are not enhancement gaps; they are access barriers. The component requires architectural revision before it can ship.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- Does the parent container mount a persistent `aria-live` region, or is this toast fully self-contained? If self-contained, conditional mounting will break live region reliability across React versions.\n- Are visual type indicators (success/error) implemented with color alone, or do they include shape/text fallbacks?\n- Is there a global toast queue manager that prevents overlapping announcements from confusing screen readers?\n\n*Note on test evidence*: No automated accessibility test results were provided. If axe-core or Pa11y runs are executed, they may flag the missing `aria-live` as a warning rather than a violation (since it's not a static HTML requirement), which could create false confidence. Manual AT testing with NVDA/JAWS/VoiceOver is required to verify live region behavior and focus management.",
